// CreateDunningRun invokes createDunningRun operation.
//
// Starts a dunning run for a company at a key date and answers its proposal (ADR-0065 §3-§5, T76a):
// per customer one notice (`GET /v1/dunning-runs/{id}/notices`) with the customer's open items overdue
// on the key date and the dunning level each would reach. The run is `proposed`: nothing changes on
// the items, and nothing is sent to a customer. The items are the open items on the company's customer
// reconciliation accounts in the leading ledger, whoever posted them. An item is proposed when the
// customer owes it (a debit) and, on the key date, it is past its net due date by at least the days of
// the company's first dunning level (`companies[].dunning.levels` of the configuration); an item due
// on the key date is not overdue. The net due date is that of the payment terms the item was posted
// with; an item posted without payment terms (a manual or migrated entry's) is due on its document
// date and is proposed like any other. Credit items (a credit memo, a payment on account) are not
// dunned and reduce nothing, with or without terms. An item with an open dispute case
// (`createDisputeCase`) is in no notice, whatever part of it is disputed: the run lists it in
// `disputed_items` with its case, and it counts in none of the run's totals; once the case is closed a
// new run proposes it again (T86b). An item with an active dunning block (`createDunningBlock`) of its
// own or of its customer, whose `valid_to` is on or after the key date, is in no notice either: the
// run lists it in `blocked_items` with its block, outside its totals, and not in `disputed_items`
// (T76e). An item a customer promised to pay (`createPromiseToPay`) by a date on or after the key date
// is in no notice either, unless blocked: the run lists it in `promised_items` with its promise,
// outside its totals, and not in `disputed_items` (T76f). An item rises one level per confirmed run at
// most, from the level the confirmed runs recorded for it (`previous_level`; `confirmDunningRun`): one
// never dunned would reach level 1, one dunned at level 1 reaches level 2 once it is overdue by the
// second level's days, and one at the highest level stays there. A notice's level is the highest among
// its items. The company's dunning procedure applies as the configuration has it when the run is
// started, and the run keeps it (T76d): an item overdue by no more than
// `companies[].dunning.grace_days` is not proposed; a customer whose notice's overdue amount would be
// below `companies[].dunning.minimum_amount` gets no notice, and the run counts it in
// `below_minimum_notice_count` and `below_minimum_amount`; a notice is charged its level's `charge`,
// and each item at a level that charges `interest` is charged interest at `interest_rate_percent` a
// year for its days overdue (`local_amount` × rate ÷ 100 × `days_overdue` ÷ 365, rounded half away
// from zero in the company's currency); a notice's `total_amount` is what it asks for in all. Charges
// and interest are what the notices ask for: nothing is posted until the run is confirmed
// (`confirmDunningRun`, T76h). A key date after 2100-12-31 is refused (422
// `collections.invalid_key_date`, naming `key_date`). A company without dunning levels is refused (422
// `collections.no_dunning_levels`), as is a run of more than 5,000 overdue items, the disputed ones it
// lists counted with them (422 `collections.run_too_large`: larger runs are not supported yet) and one
// whose amounts add up to more than an amount holds (422 `collections.amount_too_large`). A key date
// at which nothing is overdue gives a run without notices. A retry with the same `Idempotency-Key`
// answers the run started. Human-only (`x-agent: false`).
//
