// RunAnalyticsQuery invokes runAnalyticsQuery operation.
//
// Answers governed metrics (the semantic model in the active configuration version's `analytics`
// section, `semantic.yaml` until a change request adds one; metric definitions change only through
// change requests), broken down by its dimensions and filtered on them, for the companies the
// principal may read. A query names metrics and dimensions, never SQL. It is answered from the
// analytical store when the tenant's store was found current within the freshness target and no
// earlier than `as_of`, else from the database; `source` and `as_of` say which (ADR-0044). The
// database answers through the API's own reads as the caller, so a metric over data the caller may not
// read is 403 as that read is, and the store answers only a caller who holds the permissions of those
// reads; the cash actuals (metric `actual`, ADR-0051 §11) are read as `getCashActuals` classifies
// them, with `cash.forecast.read`, and name no supplier, so a query of them alone needs no
// `partner.supplier.read`; so are the forecast's metrics (`forecast_lag_0`, `forecast_lag_7`,
// `forecast_lag_30`, ADR-0051 §6), each value date's flows as the company's snapshot taken that many
// days before it kept them (its daily snapshot, else the last taken on demand that day; at document
// level, blocked suppliers' flows left out), so a week or a month adds days of different snapshots,
// unlike `getLiquidityForecastVariance`, which compares one snapshot over its period, each flow's
// source the dimension `flow_source`; and so are the forecast's variance and accuracy
// (`variance_lag_N`, `absolute_variance_lag_N`, `absolute_forecast_lag_N`, `accuracy_lag_N` for N of
// 0, 7 and 30, ADR-0051 §11), in cells of a company's value date, currency and liquidity item
// compared as that report compares them (days passed, of a snapshot taken the lag before): the
// accuracy is 1 less the weighted absolute percentage error, the cells' absolute variances over their
// absolute forecasts, rounded as that report's `variance_percent` first, so one cell's is 100% less
// its `variance_percent`, and null where nothing was forecast; actuals adding up to more than an
// amount can hold are 422 `cash.amount_too_large`. The sales metrics (`order_intake`, `billed_value`,
// `billing_document_count`, T91a) are the net amounts of the items of confirmed sales orders by order
// date and of posted billing documents by billing date, each in its document's currency and never
// converted, by the customer's number (`customer`) and name (`customer_name`), the item key
// (`item_key`) and `currency`; a draft order and a billing document not posted are not counted. They
// are read as `listSalesOrders` and `listBillingDocuments` list them, so they need `sales.order.read`
// and `sales.billing.read` both, and, since they name customers, `partner.supplier.read` as the
// vendors' names do. The spend metrics (`spend`, `spend_under_contract`, `spend_off_contract`,
// `off_contract_share`, T91b) are the net amounts of the items of posted supplier invoices by posting
// date, less those of posted credit memos (a subsequent debit adds, a subsequent credit reduces; a
// document parked, on hold, rejected, cancelled or reversed is not counted), each in its document's
// currency and never converted, by `vendor` and `vendor_name`, `account` and `account_name`,
// `cost_center` (its code), `contract` (the document number of the contract the item's purchase order
// was made on) and `currency`; an item on a purchase order has the order item's account and cost
// center, and is under contract when the order was made on a contract, while an item on no order, or
// on an order made on no contract, is off contract; `off_contract_share` is the part off contract over
// spend, null where there is none. They are read as `listSupplierInvoices`, `listPurchaseOrders`,
// `listContracts` and `listCostCenters` list them, so they need `payables.invoice.read`,
// `procurement.order.read`, `procurement.contract.read` and `ledger.cost_center.read`, with
// `partner.supplier.read` and `ledger.account.read` for the names. A tenant without a configuration is
// 409 `config.not_bootstrapped`. A query the semantic layer refuses (an unknown metric or dimension, a
// filter it cannot apply, amounts of two currencies summed) is 422 `analytics.invalid_query`; too many
// queries answering at once on the server is 503 `analytics.busy` with `Retry-After`. The result is
// JSON (`format=json`, the default) or an Excel workbook (`format=xlsx`, T95d, ADR-0076 §8): one
// sheet, a header row of the columns' names, a money measure followed by its currency's column
// (`<name>_currency`) as `getReportRun`'s CSV has it, then a row per result row; a measure is a number
// written from its exact decimal, with as many decimals shown as it has digits, and every other cell
// is text, never a formula. A result cut at the rows a query answers (`truncated`) is not written as a
// workbook, which could not say so (422 `analytics.result_truncated`: narrow the query or give it a
// `limit`). A format the contract does not list is 422 `request.validation_failed`. Changes nothing:
// agents of any autonomy level may run it.
